# Hand the laptop keyboard's lighting to the desktop user.
#
# The hyprkbd driver publishes its attributes root-writable, which is the
# right default for a kernel module but leaves the settings app -- a
# desktop application -- unable to change a colour. This rule gives them to
# the user who installed hypr-util; setup.sh substitutes the uid and gid
# below.
#
# The driver creates these files through its driver_data dev_groups, so they
# exist before this "add" event is sent and the chown cannot race them.
#
# Writing to them can set the keyboard's colours and nothing else: the ACPI
# method behind them is fixed in the module, which is the whole point of
# having one. The alternative this replaced -- write access to
# /proc/acpi/call -- would have handed the session every ACPI method on the
# machine.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="platform", KERNEL=="hyprkbd", \
  RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'chown @OWNER@ /sys%p/colors /sys%p/zone[0-3] /sys%p/backlight /sys%p/refresh 2>/dev/null; exit 0'"
